\section{Software quality assurance}{

Statistical validation and software checks are treated as separate,
complementary layers. Numerical agreement with another package does
not replace tests of raster handling, and high code coverage does not
establish statistical correctness.

\strong{Internal automated tests} cover published equations, hand-worked
examples, edge cases, invalid inputs, missing and constant series,
serial and parallel equivalence, raster geometry, stable return
structures, and user-facing reporting. Regression tests protect
previously corrected behaviour, including exact messages or object
shapes where these form part of the public interface.

\strong{Release-check protocol}:
\itemize{
\item regenerate \code{NAMESPACE} and all \code{.Rd} files with
\code{devtools::document()};
\item run \code{devtools::check(args = "--as-cran")}, with compact vignettes;
\item inspect global and per-file coverage with
\code{covr::package_coverage()} and use \code{covr::zero_coverage()} to locate
individual unexercised expressions; each gap is reviewed, because
coverage is a diagnostic rather than a substitute for numerical
validation;
\item check documentation spelling, URLs, code style, and broader package
practice with \code{spelling}, \code{urlchecker}, \code{lintr}, and \code{goodpractice};
\item run win-builder for R-devel, R-release, and R-oldrelease, and use
r-hub for additional operating systems and toolchains;
\item build the pkgdown site and manual for visual review, and inspect
packaged data with \code{tools::checkRdaFiles()};
\item record only real, recent results from those runs in
\code{cran-comments.md}; no success result is inferred from the existence
of this protocol.
}

\strong{External numerical controls} compare independent implementations
only where they calculate the same estimand:
\itemize{
\item classic Mann-Kendall against \code{Kendall} and \code{trend};
\item CMK against its published RAMK equations and the open-source
\code{ConMK} implementation;
\item regional score aggregation against \code{rkt}, without requiring its
Hirsch-Slack variance to equal CMK's analytical RAMK variance;
\item Theil-Sen against \code{trend}, repeated median against the convention
implemented by \code{robslopes}, and OLS against \code{stats};
\item prewhitening against \code{modifiedmk}, with additional source- and
output-level checks involving \code{zyp} and \code{MannKendallTrends};
\item BH/BY against \code{stats::p.adjust()} and the two-stage BKY behaviour
against the implementation documented by \code{multtest};
\item raster values, geometry, time metadata, and file round trips
through \code{terra}.
\item the simulation and benchmarking cycle against analytical covariance,
\code{fields::Matern()}, and \code{Kendall::MannKendall()} in 33 prespecified
external controls. The full recorded experiment used 1,000 fields per
spatial model and 500 paired replicates in each of eight scenarios;
every control passed. Exact scope and limitations are retained under
\verb{inst/validation/}.
}

TerrSet is retained as a complementary historical comparison rather
than ground truth because its contextual intermediate statistics are
not exposed. Reproducible external scripts and frozen results that
cannot run during \verb{R CMD check} are stored under \verb{inst/validation/}.

Additional engineering controls include:
\itemize{
\item \strong{Runtime feedback}: iterative public computations use one
dependency-free display reporting completed progress, elapsed duration
and estimated time remaining. Indivisible operations report elapsed
duration because a defensible remaining-time estimate is unavailable.
Set \code{verbose = FALSE} to suppress runtime messages.
\item \strong{API stability}: the public function names, argument names, and
argument order were audited for consistency across every exported
function (e.g. \code{report} before \code{verbose} wherever both exist,
\code{method} always immediately after the primary data argument,
\code{seed} before \code{n_cores} wherever both exist) and are considered
stable from this point forward -- breaking renames are not
expected in future releases without a compelling reason.
\item \strong{Reproducibility}: every use of randomness in this package's own
examples, tests, and simulations was audited to confirm an
explicit \code{seed} is set wherever the result actually depends on it
(calls that error out on invalid input before reaching any random
step, or that use a \code{method} with no randomness involved, are the
only exceptions, and were checked individually, not assumed safe).
\item \strong{Consistent \code{print()}/\code{summary()}/\code{plot()} styling}: every
\code{"sptrends"} subclass's \code{print()} output opens with the same
\verb{<Title...>} bracketed convention, and \code{summary()} methods
delegate to one shared underlying \verb{_summary()} function per result
type rather than duplicating formatting logic -- \code{\link[=compare_detections]{compare_detections()}}
is the one deliberate exception, since it remains an ordinary data
frame by design and is printed as one.
\item \strong{Fail-fast, specific error messages}: functions taking a
\code{SpatRaster} time series check for zero complete-time-series cells
immediately, before any computation, with a message naming what
there was nothing to do -- rather than silently continuing through
the full computation and eventually erroring (or worse, returning
\code{NA}/\code{NaN}-filled output without erroring at all) somewhere
downstream. Two real, previously-unguarded degenerate cases were
found and fixed this way: \code{\link[=spatial_autocorrelation]{spatial_autocorrelation()}} returning an
uncaught, silent \code{NaN} for a perfectly constant raster, and
\code{\link[=sim_trend_stack]{sim_trend_stack()}} dividing by zero for a 1x1 grid under two of
its three \code{trend_shape} options.
\item \strong{Performance}: reviewed for genuinely costly patterns (nested
per-cell loops, repeated raster read/write round trips), not
optimised indiscriminately at the expense of readability.
\code{\link[=sim_trend_stack]{sim_trend_stack()}} retains its batched legacy focal smoother for
compatibility, while formal Gaussian, exponential and Matérn fields use
circulant embedding and FFT rather than dense covariance matrices.
\item \strong{Memory model}: the principal analytical functions currently
materialise the complete cell-by-time matrix with \code{terra::values()}.
Vectorisation, sparse adjacency and optional PSOCK parallelism reduce
runtime, but do not make the algorithms out-of-core. Peak memory grows
with \code{ncell(x) * nlyr(x)} and can include several matrices of that
size, especially for CMK and prewhitening. A reproducible scaling
benchmark is installed at \code{inst/benchmarks/scalability.R}; users should
test representative dimensions before processing very large rasters.
\item \strong{External validation}: this package's core statistics are
checked, on real output rather than by inspection alone, against
independent, long-established CRAN implementations of the same
published methods -- the Mann-Kendall S statistic against
\code{Kendall::MannKendall()} and \code{trend::mk.test()}, the Theil-Sen
slope against \code{trend::sens.slope()}, and Yue-Pilon (2002)
trend-free prewhitening's own pre-prewhitening slope against
\code{modifiedmk::tfpwmk()} (confirmed, by reading its source directly,
to implement the same published algorithm this package's own
\code{prewhiten(method = "TFPW_Y")} does). Deterministic quantities
with only one correct value (S, the Theil-Sen slope) matched
these external packages exactly, down to a difference of \code{0}, not
merely approximately; the p-value/z-statistic is checked only
approximately, since different packages make different,
individually legitimate choices about continuity correction and
the variance-of-S formula under ties. See
\code{test-external-validation.R} in the package's own test suite for
the full detail.
\item \strong{Consistent visual identity across every map this package
draws}: diverging palettes (Theil-Sen slope, the S/Sm trend
statistic, direction-of-change results) always use
\code{hcl.colors(50, "Blue-Red 3")}, deliberately colourblind-safer
and domain-neutral -- not \code{"Green-Brown"}, which reads naturally
as vegetation greening/browning but would be a strange default
for, say, a temperature or precipitation series; that palette
remains available and worth using explicitly for NDVI-like data
specifically (see \code{?trend_maps}'s and \code{?workflow_rta}'s own
\verb{@examples} for exactly that). Every diverging map's own colour
range is capped at 2 standard deviations rather than the single
most extreme cell (\code{.robust_diverging_range()}, internal), so one
outlier cannot wash out everyone else's colour into a narrow,
barely-distinguishable band near the palette's midpoint. Purely
binary significant/non-significant results (\code{fdr_significance_maps()},
\code{fdr_threshold_plot()}) use this package's own official brand
navy (\code{"#071D4B"}, one of a fixed 5-colour palette --
\code{.sptrends_brand}, internal -- taken from the exact colours used
across every version of \code{man/figures/logo.png}, not re-derived
from the image itself) for "significant" against a neutral grey
for "not significant" -- the one colour a reader learns to
recognise as this package's consistent "yes" signal, wherever it
appears.
}
}

